WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.558
"Food Poisoning:
Causes and Prevention"

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.080
Remember that 24-hour flu
you had last year?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.680
Well, there is no such thing
as a 24- or 48-hour flu.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.720
There is no such thing
as stomach flu.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.560
What you had
is likely food poisoning.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.040
When someone gets hepatitis
from eating a strawberry,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.320
the hepatitis didn't come
from the strawberry;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.480
berries don't have
little livers.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.320
As Dr. McDougall liked to ask,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.320
when is the last time
we heard of someone getting

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.240
Dutch elm disease
or a bad case of aphids?

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.520
Just as virtually
all known human pandemics

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.560
come from animals originally,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.480
so does most food poisoning,
specifically animal feces,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.320
and that manure runoff
can contaminate

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.240
sprout seeds, spinach,
and other healthy plant foods.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.960
Still, that's better
than eating the manure directly.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.080
Animal products,
particularly fish and poultry,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.960
can be covered
in fecal bacteria.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.760
It's so bad that while
the U.S. federal government

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.280
recommends that we wash
our fruits and vegetables,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.440
we're not supposed
to rinse meat

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.160
for fear of the viral
and bacterial splatter.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.680
It's even been put to the test,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.000
and indeed,
washing chicken in the sink

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.520
under standard
kitchen faucet conditions

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.360
can spew droplets containing
culturable levels of pathogens

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.080
throughout the kitchen.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.800
Meat is so covered
in fecal matter residue

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.360
that researchers found more
fecal matter in the kitchen--

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.280
on sponges, dish towels,
and sink drains--

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.760
than they found
swabbing the toilet.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.680
Even after repeatedly cleaning
everything with bleach,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.600
it may be safer to lick
the rim of the toilet seat

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.640
than the kitchen countertop,

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.200
because people aren't preparing
chickens in their toilets.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.160
That chicken "juice"
in the cellophane

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.240
is more like fecal soup.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.000
As described by the head
of the food inspectors' union:

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.920
"You take a chicken
that eats, sleeps, craps,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.320
and everything else
in one space,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.400
they enter the scald vat dirty,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.560
and it takes only a few minutes

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.680
to become just
brown fecal soup."

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.920
And that's what leaks
out of the chicken later

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.120
in the meat case.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.120
No wonder poultry accounts for
more food poisoning outbreaks

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.920
than any other food.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.240
And, according to the U.S.
General Accounting Office,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.920
contaminated poultry products
also cause the most deaths.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.440
This is because two
of the most significant causes

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.920
of hospitalizations and death,
Campylobacter and Salmonella,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.840
are found in poultry.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.880
Campylobacter is a fecal poultry
pathogen that can trigger

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.240
something called
Guillain-Barré syndrome,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.160
where you come down

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.160
with Campylobacter
food poisoning once,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.040
and then you can end up
paralyzed on a ventilator.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.120
You're not in a coma;
you're awake,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.960
but you can be
so completely paralyzed

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.320
you can't even
breathe on your own,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.480
like straight out
of a buried-alive horror movie.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.160
According to the
U.S. Department of Agriculture,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.480
nearly half of chicken quarters
or halves in the United States

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.360
are contaminated
with this pathogen.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.280
But Campylobacter isn't
the leading cause

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.440
of food poisoning
hospitalizations.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.760
That's Salmonella,

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.760
which is also the leading cause
of foodborne death.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.440
An investigation
by Consumer Reports

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.040
found widespread
Salmonella contamination;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.280
31 percent
of ground chicken samples

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.080
taken from grocery stores
across the U.S.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.640
were found to be tainted
with Salmonella.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.040
All the Salmonella they found

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.560
was resistant
to at least one antibiotic,

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.320
and 78 percent were resistant
to multiple drugs.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.720
That's one of the reasons
Salmonella is the leading reason

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.800
people can die
from a single meal.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.480
It's difficult to treat
human Salmonella infections

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.880
because they're
becoming resistant

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.040
to so many of our
miracle drug antibiotics.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.600
After the Jack in the Box
E. coli outbreak

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:43.760
in the early nineties,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.320
E. coli O157:H7 was officially
deemed an adulterant,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.800
meaning contaminated beef
could not be legally sold

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.040
and has to be recalled.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.400
But the USDA has not taken
similar action

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.360
to protect the public
from Salmonella,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.960
which is widespread
in chicken,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.360
thanks to the often crowded
and filthy conditions

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.040
in which they are raised.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.520
More than a million Americans
get sick from Salmonella

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.480
every year;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.800
that's about five times more
than E. coli sickens,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.520
yet the USDA looks the other way

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.800
if less than one in six chicken
parts is contaminated,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.440
and even when those levels
are exceeded,

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.160
the meat still ends up
in grocery stores.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.560
So, when you actually test
retail chicken parts,

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.480
more than 40 percent can be
contaminated with Salmonella.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.440
Why is it that European nations
can boast so much lower

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.640
Salmonella contamination?

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.480
Because in Europe,
they require that their meat

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.600
is Salmonella free.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.320
They still find some
Salmonella-contaminated flocks,

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.680
but the difference is that
it is illegal to sell

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.080
Salmonella-positive
chicken there.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.640
Banning contaminated poultry
is a "hard-handed" policy,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.080
an Alabama poultry science
professor explains:

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.560
"The fact is
that it's just too expensive

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.320
not to sell
Salmonella-positive chicken."

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.560
Let that sink in for a moment.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.160
That's like
a toy manufacturer saying,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.000
"Sorry, we'd love to pull
the unsafe toys from the market,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.840
but such a large percentage
of our toys are hazardous,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.000
that would just be
too expensive."

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.720
In 2023, the USDA at least
proposed that we shouldn't allow

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.760
Salmonella in products like raw
frozen breaded poultry products

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.760
that consumers may undercook.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.720
What did the National Chicken
Council have to say?

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:33.040
The poultry industry
was gravely concerned.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.120
Not about all the graves
created by their products,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.760
but the "200 million
servings of this product

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.600
[that] will be lost."

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.280
This is the poultry industry
admitting

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.880
that they are producing hundreds
of millions of servings

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.640
of Salmonella-contaminated
chicken every year.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.800
In terms of fish hygiene,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.680
researchers swabbed sushi
for fecal bacteria.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.480
Nearly all samples violated

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.840
international
food safety standards.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.200
They also tested cucumber
and avocado rolls

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.600
and found
no fecal contamination,

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:06.280
perhaps because, unlike salmon,
tuna, and shrimp,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.800
fruits and vegetables
don't have rectums.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.760
Though the E. coli
may not necessarily

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.480
come from fish butts,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.400
as higher E. coli counts
were found

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.920
when sushi was prepared
with bare hands,

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.000
compared to gloved.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.840
What percentage of other meat
may come prepackaged with poop?

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.231
Anywhere from
a quarter to two thirds.

